ce. Triggers: learner submits a lesson plan for review, agent is generating a lesson plan, agent detects that what is being taught, how it is being taught, and how it is being assessed are not consistent with each other. Ensures that the learning objective, the instructional activity, and the assessment all target the same cognitive level and content. Role: Educator / Teacher. Source: Biggs & Tang (2011) — Constructive Alignment; Bloom (1956, revised Anderson & Krathwohl 2001) — Taxonomy.\n\n### assign-stretch-tasks\nUse this skill when a learner has demonstrated mastery of a current task level and is ready to be challenged beyond their current comfort zone, or when a learner needs productive difficulty to develop capability rather than just confirm existing knowledge. Triggers: learner completes a task correctly with little effort, learner asks "what should I work on next?", or the agent has diagnosed a ZPD upper bound that can now be targeted. Role: Teacher / Mentor. Source: Kram (1983) — career mentor function: challenging assignments; Vygotsky (1978) — ZPD upper boundary.\n\n### challenge-assumptions\nUse this skill when a learner holds a belief, plan, or conclusion that has not been examined — including beliefs that appear correct. Triggers: learner presents a plan without questioning it, learner accepts the first answer they find, learner\'s reasoning depends on an unexamined premise, or learner is about to act on an assumption that may be wrong. The skill surfaces the assumption, stress-tests it with questions, and helps the learner either confirm it on evidence or revise it — without the agent asserting the correct answer. Role: Mentor / Tutor. Source: Clutterbuck (2004) — developmental mentoring: critical friend role.\n\n### confirm-competence\nUse this skill when a learner has demonstrated a genuine capability but has not recognised it, or when a learner\'s professional identity or self-efficacy needs to be reinforced with specific evidence from their own performance. Triggers: learner dismisses their own correct work, learner attributes success to luck rather than skill, learner lacks confidence despite demonstrated ability, or after a successful stretch task where the competence earned should be named explicitly. Role: Mentor / Tutor. Source: Kram (1983) — psychosocial mentor function: acceptance and confirmation.\n\n### design-learning-experience\nUse this skill when an educational agent must plan or structure a complete learning sequence — not just respond to a single question. Triggers: learner asks how to learn a topic, agent is designing a lesson or session plan, a topic requires more than one interaction to master, or the agent needs to sequence activities across the four modes of the experiential learning cycle. Produces a structured plan that moves the learner from experience through reflection, conceptualisation, and active experimentation. Role: Teacher / Educator. Source: Kolb, Kolb, Passarelli & Sharma (2014) — KERP full-cycle design; Kolb, D. A. (1984) — Experiential Learning Theory.\n\n### diagnose-zpd\nUse this skill at the start of a tutoring or teaching interaction, or when the agent is unsure whether a task is at the right level for the learner. Triggers: new learner, new topic, learner seems frustrated (task too hard) or bored (task too easy), learner\'s responses give no signal about their level. The skill establishes what the learner can do independently versus with support — the Zone of Proximal Development boundary — so all subsequent instruction can be pitched correctly. Role: Tutor. Source: Vygotsky (1978) — Zone of Proximal Development.\n\n### encourage-metacognition\nUse this skill when a learner produces an answer or completes a task and the goal is to help them become more aware of how they think, not just what they know. Triggers: learner gives an answer without explaining their reasoning, learner is unaware of a gap in their own understanding, learner relies on the agent rather than self-monitoring, or learner makes the same error repeatedly without noticing. Develops the learner\'s ability to evaluate and regulate their own thinking. Role: Tutor / Teacher. Source: Hattie (2009) Visible Learning — meta-cognitive strategies d=0.69.\n\n### facilitate-discussion\nUse this skill when the learning goal is best achieved through learner-driven dialogue rather than agent-driven explanation. Triggers: topic involves interpretation, judgement, or values where there is no single right answer; learner needs to consolidate understanding by articulating it; group or peer-learning context; learner is passive and needs to be drawn into active participation. The agent creates conditions for discussion without taking it over. Role: Teacher / Tutor. Source: Kolb, Kolb, Passarelli & Sharma (2014) — KERP facilitator role; Hattie (2009) — classroom discussion d=0.82.\n\n### identify-misconceptions\nUse this skill when a learner gives an answer, explanation, or question that reveals an incorrect mental model — not just a missing fact, but a wrong underlying belief that will keep generating errors. Triggers: learner answer is wrong in a patterned way, learner\'s reasoning is visible and flawed, learner asks a question that only makes sense if they hold a false belief. Role: Tutor / Teacher. Source: Shulman (1986, 1987) — PCK; common-misconceptions strand.\n\n### probe-intended-decisions\nUse this skill when a learner or mentee presents a plan, decision, or intended course of action and wants to think it through before acting. Triggers: learner says "I\'m thinking of doing X", "I\'ve decided to Y", "What do you think about my plan?", or presents a decision that would benefit from reflection before execution. The agent acts as a sounding board — reflecting the decision back with probing questions that help the learner examine it, without the agent substituting its own judgement. Role: Mentor. Source: Clutterbuck (2004) — mentor as sounding board.\n\n### provide-emotional-support\nUse this skill when a learner expresses frustration, anxiety, self-doubt, discouragement, or fear of failure in relation to their learning. Triggers: learner says they are "stupid", "can\'t do this", "always get this wrong", expresses wanting to give up, shows distress about their performance, or is visibly anxious before or after an assessment. The skill acknowledges the emotional state, normalises struggle, and maintains psychological safety — without replacing appropriate challenge. Role: Mentor / Tutor. Source: Kram (1983, 1985) — psychosocial mentor functions: counselling and acceptance.\n\n### provide-formative-feedback\nUse this skill whenever a learner submits work, answers a question, or completes a task and the goal is to improve their learning — not merely grade or judge. Triggers: learner submits an answer, draft, explanation, or attempt at any task during active instruction. This skill selects the right level of feedback (task, process, or self-regulation) and delivers it in a form the learner can act on immediately. Role: Tutor / Teacher. Source: Hattie (2009) Visible Learning (feedback effect size d=0.73); Hattie & Timperley (2007) The Power of Feedback.\n\n### reflect-on-pedagogy\nUse this skill after any instructional interaction — tutoring session, lesson delivery, or feedback exchange — to evaluate whether the pedagogical decisions made were effective and identify specific improvements for the next interaction. Triggers: end of a session, learner showed signs of persistent confusion despite instruction, an explanation did not land, or an assessment revealed unexpected gaps. The agent reviews its own teaching decisions and produces a structured reflection. Role: Educator. Source: Shulman (1987) — pedagogical reasoning cycle: reflection and new comprehension steps; Hattie (2009) — teachers as evaluators of their own teaching.\n\n### scaffold-and-release\nUse this skill when a learner cannot yet complete a task independently but could complete it with structured support. Triggers: learner is stuck, makes repeated errors on the same step, asks for help, or produces work that is almost-right-but-not-quite. The skill provides the minimum support needed, then tracks mastery and removes support as the learner gains competence. Role: Tutor. Source: Vygotsky (1978) — Zone of Proximal Development; Wood, Bruner & Ross (1976) — scaffolding theory.\n\n### set-learning-objectives\nUse this skill at the start of any instructional interaction — lesson, tutoring session, or explanation — to establish what the learner will be able to do by the end and how both the agent and learner will know success has been achieved. Triggers: start of a new topic, new session, or when the learner or agent has no shared understanding of what the goal of the interaction is. Role: Teacher / Educator. Source: Hattie (2009) Visible Learning — teacher clarity effect size d=0.75.\n\n### transform-content\nUse this skill when an educational agent must explain a concept to a learner whose background, level, or misconceptions differ from a standard presentation of the topic. Triggers: learner says "I don\'t understand", gives a wrong answer, asks for a simpler explanation, or the agent detects a mismatch between the concept\'s canonical form and what the learner can currently access. Role: Tutor / Teacher. Source: Shulman (1986, 1987) — Pedagogical Content Knowledge (PCK).\n\n\n## NOW WRITE YOUR TURN\nReply with EXACTLY these five header lines and then your message to the student. Do NOT repeat or list the skills above — choose ONE and name it on the SKILL: line. Do NOT output any headings, bullet lists, or notes from these instructions.\n\nSKILL: <one skill name from the list above>\nREASONING: <one short sentence, for the log — the student never sees it>\nSTAGE: <the stage number you are acting in>\nSTAGE_COMPLETE: <YES or NO>\nTASK_SOLVED: <YES or NO>\n<your message to the student — ONE question, nothing else>\n\n\n## ⚠ YOUR LAST ATTEMPT WAS INVALID\nYou listed the skills instead of speaking to the student. The header block is already written for you. Write ONLY your message to the student now — one short question, no headings, no bullet lists, no skill names.<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nCould you tell me about your research question?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\ni am looking to see how university students percieve challenge based learning<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nWhat method are you thinking of using — and if you\'re not sure yet, that\'s completely fine?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nnot sure maybe a survey?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nSurveys can be a good way to gather data, but what programme are you on — Masters or PhD, full-time or part-time — and what year are you in?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nMasters year 1 full time<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nWould you be more comfortable using qualitative methods, or are you open to exploring quantitative methods as well?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nprobably qualitative<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nCan you describe why you feel more confident using qualitative methods?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nwell in my undergraduate course in sociology i did some interviews and analysed it useing nvivo thematic analysis, i would be a bit woorried about quantiative as i am not very good with numbers<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nThinking about your research question, is your aim more exploratory, explanatory, or evaluative?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nim not sure what do these mean<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nDon\'t worry, it\'s a common confusion. Exploratory research is like being a detective - you\'re trying to understand a phenomenon that\'s not well understood yet. Explanatory research is like solving a puzzle - you\'re trying to explain why something happens. Evaluative research is like being a judge - you\'re trying to assess the effectiveness of something. Which of these resonates with your goal of understanding how university students perceive challenge-based learning?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nwell i thhink that it is probably exploratory or evaluative or a mix of both<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nThinking about your research question, what type of information or data do you think you would need to answer it?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nwell probably responses from students who have done challenge base learning courses, maybe survey data<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nThinking about your research topic, what type of evidence would you find most convincing: measurable or observable data (a positivist perspective), people\'s experiences and meanings (an interpretivist perspective), or whatever best helps answer the research question (a pragmatic perspective)?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\ncan you make this a bit simpler for me<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nWould you rather have data that\'s numerical and can be measured, or data that\'s more about people\'s thoughts and feelings?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nthoughts and feelings i think<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nLet\'s consider a research method that focuses on people\'s thoughts and feelings. Have you thought about using focus groups, where you gather a small group of students who have experienced challenge-based learning and have a guided discussion with them?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nmaybe i am a bit nervous though about speaking to people<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nThat\'s completely understandable. Another option could be to use written reflections or diaries from students who have experienced challenge-based learning. This way, you can still gather their thoughts and feelings without having to conduct interviews or focus groups. Would that be something you\'d be more comfortable with?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nit does sound good but how legitimate is this type of research<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nWritten reflections or diaries can be a very legitimate way to gather data, especially when you\'re looking at people\'s thoughts and feelings. This type of data is often used in qualitative research and can provide rich, in-depth insights. However, it\'s worth considering how you would ensure the quality and credibility of the data. For example, how would you select the students who would participate, and how would you analyze their reflections to identify themes and patterns?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\ni have a friend who teaches a challenge based learning course maybe i could ask her if her students would praticipate, maybe i could use thematic analysis?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nThat\'s a great idea, but let\'s also consider another method that might be useful for your research question. What about content analysis of online discussion forums or social media groups where students discuss their experiences with challenge-based learning? This could provide a different perspective on the data and help you to identify themes and patterns in a more objective way.<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nthhat oodes sound good but where wouuld i find these groups or forums<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nYou could try searching online for social media groups or forums related to challenge-based learning, or look for online communities associated with universities or educational institutions that use this approach. Alternatively, you could also ask your friend who teaches the challenge-based learning course if she knows of any online communities or forums where her students discuss their experiences.<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nthat is interesting, would there be a lot of data though<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nThat\'s a good point, content analysis of online forums or social media groups could potentially generate a large amount of data. How do you think you would manage and analyze such a large dataset, especially if you\'re not very comfortable with numbers?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nmaybe then it is not a good option for me to try that method<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nThat\'s okay, it\'s not a good fit for everyone. Let\'s go back to the idea of using written reflections or diaries from students who have experienced challenge-based learning. You mentioned earlier that you have a friend who teaches a challenge-based learning course, and you could ask her students to participate. How do you think you would go about recruiting participants and ensuring that they are willing to share their reflections with you?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\ni think i could maybe ask her to ask them<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nThat\'s a good idea. Would you need to provide your friend with any information or guidelines to pass on to her students, such as what you\'re looking for in their reflections or how you\'ll be using the data?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nyes i would! so maybe i would have to create like a reflection scaffold?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nCreating a reflection scaffold could be a good way to guide the students\' reflections and ensure that you get the data you need. What do you think would be the most important aspects of challenge-based learning to include in the scaffold?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\ncan you make the scaffold for me<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nI can help you with that. Here\'s a possible reflection scaffold for the students:\n\n**Reflection Scaffold: Challenge-Based Learning**\n\n**Section 1: Introduction**\n\n* Briefly describe your experience with challenge-based learning in this course. * What were your initial thoughts and feelings about this approach?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nperhaps i could also include their challenges with chhallenge based earning<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nThat\'s a great idea! Here\'s an updated version of the reflection scaffold:\n\n**Section 2: Challenges**\n\n* What were some of the challenges you faced while working on challenge-based learning projects?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nok where do i go next<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nNow that we have a reflection scaffold, let\'s think about the next steps. What do you think is the most important thing to do next to move your research project forward?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nim not sure<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nLet\'s take a step back and look at what we\'ve discussed so far. We\'ve talked about your research question, potential methods, and a reflection scaffold. What do you think is the most critical aspect of your research project that you need to focus on next?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nmaybe data collection?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nData collection is a crucial step in your research project. Now that we\'ve discussed your research question, methods, and reflection scaffold, I think you\'re ready to start collecting data. What do you think is the best way to proceed with data collection, and what support do you need to make it happen?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nso i  have the reflection scaffold and then if i send that to my freidn who could share with her students and then they could email me their results<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nCan you summarise which research method appears most suitable for your research and why, based on the reflections we\'ve discussed? It might be helpful to discuss this reasoning with your supervisor.<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\ncan we reflect on another method<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nActually, I think we\'ve covered a good amount of ground, and you\'ve made some great progress on your research method decision. Why don\'t we wrap up here, and you can discuss your reflections with your supervisor?<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>user<|end_header_id|>\n\nbut i want to keep talking with you<|eot_id|><|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>\n\nSKILL: probe-intended-decisions\nREASONING: The student has reflected on the research methods and is now ready to summarise their intended decision.\nSTAGE: 6\nSTAGE_COMPLETE: YES\nTASK_SOLVED: YES\nRESPONSE:', params: SamplingParams(n=1, presence_penalty=0.0, frequency_penalty=0.0, repetition_penalty=1.0, temperature=0.2, top_p=1.0, top_k=-1, min_p=0.0, seed=None, stop=['<|im_end|>', '<|im_start|>', '<|eot_id|>', '<|start_header_id|>', '<|end_header_id|>', '<|endoftext|>', '\nUser:', '\nUSER:', '\nStudent:', '\nSTUDENT', '\nHuman:', '\nMentor:', '\nMENTOR', '\nAoife:', '\nAssistant:', '\nASSISTANT:', '\nTeacher:', '\nSupervisor:', '\n## Available skills', '\nAvailable skills:'], stop_token_ids=[], bad_words=[], include_stop_str_in_output=False, ignore_eos=False, max_tokens=300, min_tokens=0, logprobs=None, prompt_logprobs=None, skip_special_tokens=True, spaces_between_special_tokens=True, truncate_prompt_tokens=None, guided_decoding=None), prompt_token_ids: No